f" "q-122651/selection/a4cd6ba6497a8ed699ec5f61fba3dc3b142636de86c6fbb28e5610821b13800a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122651" :question/id #uuid "31270881-eb16-5f34-9cb7-b40b052fc37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122651" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067637/assets/7cb4dd61f9d42a48c022a183e7f4310a8a4ed7ae881edd136881ef6863282e2c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=x\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y,z)=2\\,i.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-122651/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122651"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122670/selection/421ba3cf61c86c7d854403490925f53920cd3c015c12342698571d9a74aca2fd" :answer/id #uuid "81e3b2c1-267e-5ed5-b025-8f653c93fd4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122670/selection/f1aa67be50b97932b48fdfaee84cd81f0d54fb18f524a006927a5377ea4d957f" :answer/id #uuid "43ae4009-8ad5-5429-9309-0a024fc1